# Line endings: text is LF in the repository AND in every working copy, on every platform.
#
# There is deliberately no CRLF exception for .bat/.cmd. Verified on Windows 11 (build 26200): an LF-only
# batch file behaves identically to a CRLF one for goto, call :label, for loops, multi-line if (...) blocks,
# delayed expansion, exit codes, and a label as the final line with no trailing newline - and Gradle's own
# gradlew.bat runs unmodified with LF endings.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never translate these.
#
# `text=auto` decides text vs binary by looking for a NUL byte in the first 8000 bytes only. A PDF written
# by a Python tool has none, so git guesses "text" and inserts CR bytes on checkout, shifting every internal
# offset and breaking the xref table. That is not hypothetical: it corrupted 26 PDFs in guitar-tap-project's
# Windows working copies, and pdfarranger records the same incident in its own .gitattributes. PNG/WAV/JPEG
# escape only by luck - they happen to carry a NUL early - so they are listed too.
#
# Last matching rule wins, so every rule below must stay under `* text=auto eol=lf`.

# Documents
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pptx binary
*.odt binary
*.ods binary

